  "enhanced_information": "Bile produced by the liver is stored in the gallbladder and released into the duodenum primarily in response to hormonal signals, specifically cholecystokinin (CCK). CCK is released when fatty acids and amino acids enter the duodenum, stimulating gallbladder contraction and bile secretion. This process is also facilitated by parasympathetic activation, which enhances gallbladder contraction. Other hormones like secretin primarily stimulate pancreatic secretion and are not directly involved in gallbladder contraction. Therefore, the correct option is 'Cholecystokinin and parasympathetic activation.' Evidence supports that CCK causes fluid secretion and vasodilation in the duodenum, further confirming its role in digestion."
